  "affectedOriginalProse": [
    {
      "number": "829",
      "text": "829. 用两种方法求表示式$6\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}}$的值：\n1) 从平方根表里找出$\\sqrt{0.3333}$的值以后，把结果乘以6；\n2) 把$6\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}}$写成$\\sqrt{12}$，再从平方根表里找出这个式子的值。 这两种方法当中，用哪一种方法更方便？",
      "figure_ids": []
    },
    {
      "number": "830",
      "text": "830. 查平方根表求下列各式的值：\na) $4\\sqrt{5}$；\nb) $15\\sqrt{2}$；\nc) $14\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{7}}$；\nd) $12\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{6}}$。",
      "figure_ids": []
    },
    {
      "number": "831",
      "text": "831. 计算圆的面积的公式是$S=\\pi r^2$，其中$r$是圆的半径。\n用$S$表示变量$r$，再查表求圆的半径。设：\na) $S\\approx12.5\\mathrm{cm}^2$；\nb) $S\\approx8.24\\mathrm{cm}^2$。 取$\\pi\\approx3.14$。 如果圆的面积增加到$4$倍或减小到$1/25$，半径$r$怎样变化？",
      "figure_ids": []
    }
  ],
  "conceptSelectorsOnly": [
    6,
    7
  ],
  "seedCanonicalSha256": "b09b1b3cd77b2721598378c273403291ac0863758200f6a5539182263866d6e7",
  "views": {
    "schema": "STEMROBIN-182/affected-reviewer-views@1",
    "seed_sha256": "b09b1b3cd77b2721598378c273403291ac0863758200f6a5539182263866d6e7",
    "draft_sha256": "db0819e76a9eb146bf14ca6f803b4c7b72d4b3826356f24123da477a3c3fe5c3",
    "seedPath": "/Users/yong/.codex/worktrees/d9b1/lemmadeck/.intentfold/tickets/STEMROBIN-182/tmp/resume182-13/n40-choice-affected-author/RECOVERY-BINDING/seed.json",
    "sourceRepoForStructuralValidation": "/Users/yong/.codex/worktrees/d9b1/lemmadeck",
    "questions": [
      {
        "id": "n40-lookup-method-comparison",
        "source_numbers": [
          "829"
        ],
        "view": {
          "mode": "single",
          "stem": "Evaluate $6\\sqrt{\\frac13}$ by two table routes: route 1 looks up $\\sqrt{0.3333}$ and multiplies the reported root by $6$; route 2 first rewrites the exact expression as $\\sqrt{12}$ and looks it up. Which option gives the complete computed comparison and identifies the route needing fewer table/correction/rescaling operations?\n\nFor these items, use the following transcribed Table IV cells from Bradis's Four-figure Mathematical Tables (2010). Decimal commas are written as decimal points. A row N below 10 uses main-column digit j for argument N+j/100 and correction-column digit k for the extra argument k/1000. A row N at least 10 uses j/10 and k/100 instead. Add 0.001 times the correction-cell integer to the main root entry. A dash means no correction is needed. Normalize any out-of-range positive argument as a*10^(2m), with 1 <= a < 100, and scale the table root by 10^m.\n\n| Row N | Main column j | Main root entry | Correction column k | Correction integer |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 12 | 0 | 3.464 | - | - |\n| 33 | 3 | 5.771 | 3 | 3 |\n\nCompute both routes using these cells. Keep the first route's product before any final rounding; then compare both reports at three decimal places. Judge convenience by the number of required table/correction/rescaling operations.",
          "figure_ids": [],
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "a",
              "text": "Route 1: $3.4626$ before final rounding; route 2: $3.464$. Their three-decimal reports are $(3.463,\\ 3.464)$; route 2 needs fewer operations.",
              "figure_ids": []
            },
            {
              "id": "b",
              "text": "Route 1: $3.4644$ before final rounding; route 2: $3.464$. Their three-decimal reports are $(3.464,\\ 3.464)$; route 2 needs fewer operations.",
              "figure_ids": []
            },
            {
              "id": "c",
              "text": "Route 1: $0.34644$ before final rounding; route 2: $3.464$. Their three-decimal reports are $(0.346,\\ 3.464)$; route 2 needs fewer operations.",
              "figure_ids": []
            },
            {
              "id": "d",
              "text": "Route 1: $3.4644$ before final rounding; route 2: $3.464$. Their three-decimal reports are $(3.464,\\ 3.464)$; route 1 needs fewer operations.",
              "figure_ids": []
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "n40-table-ready-plan",
        "source_numbers": [
          "830"
        ],
        "view": {
          "mode": "single",
          "stem": "Use the square-root table to evaluate $4\\sqrt5$, $15\\sqrt2$, $14\\sqrt{\\frac17}$ and $12\\sqrt{\\frac56}$, in that order. Which tuple gives all four reported numerical values after the required exact preparation and root rescaling?\n\nFor these items, use the following transcribed Table IV cells from Bradis's Four-figure Mathematical Tables (2010). Decimal commas are written as decimal points. A row N below 10 uses main-column digit j for argument N+j/100 and correction-column digit k for the extra argument k/1000. A row N at least 10 uses j/10 and k/100 instead. Add 0.001 times the correction-cell integer to the main root entry. A dash means no correction is needed. Normalize any out-of-range positive argument as a*10^(2m), with 1 <= a < 100, and scale the table root by 10^m.\n\n| Row N | Main column j | Main root entry |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1.2 | 0 | 1.095 |\n| 4.5 | 0 | 2.121 |\n| 28 | 0 | 5.292 |\n| 80 | 0 | 8.944 |\n\nFirst absorb each positive outside coefficient into its square root, then normalize the radicand to 1 <= a < 100. Use these main entries and report the scaled products without further rounding.",
          "figure_ids": [],
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "a",
              "text": "$(8.944,\\ 2.121,\\ 5.292,\\ 1.095)$",
              "figure_ids": []
            },
            {
              "id": "b",
              "text": "$(8.944,\\ 21.21,\\ 5.292,\\ 10.95)$",
              "figure_ids": []
            },
            {
              "id": "c",
              "text": "$(8.944,\\ 212.1,\\ 5.292,\\ 109.5)$",
              "figure_ids": []
            },
            {
              "id": "d",
              "text": "$(8.944,\\ 10.95,\\ 5.292,\\ 21.21)$",
              "figure_ids": []
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "n40-radius-and-area",
        "source_numbers": [
          "831"
        ],
        "view": {
          "mode": "single",
          "stem": "A circle has area $S=\\pi r^2$ and positive radius $r$. Use $\\pi\\approx3.14$ for areas $S_1\\approx12.5\\,\\mathrm{cm}^2$ and $S_2\\approx8.24\\,\\mathrm{cm}^2$. Which complete result gives $r$ in terms of $S$, both table-derived radii in order, and the radius multipliers when the area is multiplied by $4$ and by $\\frac1{25}$, respectively?\n\nFor these items, use the following transcribed Table IV cells from Bradis's Four-figure Mathematical Tables (2010). Decimal commas are written as decimal points. A row N below 10 uses main-column digit j for argument N+j/100 and correction-column digit k for the extra argument k/1000. A row N at least 10 uses j/10 and k/100 instead. Add 0.001 times the correction-cell integer to the main root entry. A dash means no correction is needed. Normalize any out-of-range positive argument as a*10^(2m), with 1 <= a < 100, and scale the table root by 10^m.\n\n| Row N | Main column j | Main root entry | Correction column k | Correction integer |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 2.6 | 2 | 1.619 | 4 | 1 |\n| 3.9 | 8 | 1.995 | 1 | 0 |\n\nFor this item, round S/3.14 to four significant figures before addressing the table. Apply the captured correction and report the returned positive radius to three decimal places in cm. This input-rounding policy is a condition of this item.",
          "figure_ids": [],
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "a",
              "text": "$r=\\frac S\\pi$; $(r_1,r_2)\\approx(3.981,\\ 2.624)\\,\\mathrm{cm}$; radius multipliers $(4,\\ \\frac1{25})$.",
              "figure_ids": []
            },
            {
              "id": "b",
              "text": "$r=\\sqrt{\\frac S\\pi}$; $(r_1,r_2)\\approx(1.995,\\ 1.620)\\,\\mathrm{cm}$; radius multipliers $(2,\\ \\frac15)$.",
              "figure_ids": []
            },
            {
              "id": "c",
              "text": "$r=\\sqrt{\\frac S\\pi}$; $(r_1,r_2)\\approx(1.995,\\ 1.619)\\,\\mathrm{cm}$; radius multipliers $(2,\\ \\frac15)$.",
              "figure_ids": []
            },
            {
              "id": "d",
              "text": "$r=\\sqrt{\\frac S\\pi}$; $(r_1,r_2)\\approx(1.995,\\ 1.620)\\,\\mathrm{cm}$; radius multipliers $(4,\\ \\frac1{25})$.",
              "figure_ids": []
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
